So after enough runs, brute-force search becomes less necessary.

The system starts acquiring something resembling strategic instinct:

"When terrain looks like X,
Mirror → Portal → Build Tool
usually beats direct attack."

Then compress that successful trajectory:

NEW_SEAL = compress(
    ["Mirror", "Portal", "Build Tool"]
)

And now three moves become one callable bootloader.

The full loop is therefore:

TARGET
  ↓
BEYOND TARGET
  ↓
COURSE OF FIRE
  ↓
ORDER EFFECTS
  ↓
SIMULATED FUTURES
  ↓
CHOOSE TRAJECTORY
  ↓
EXECUTE
  ↓
AFTERGLOW
  ↓
METAMAP LEARNS
  ↓
ROUTE FOSSIL
  ↓
COMPOUND SEAL
  ↓
BETTER FUTURE SEARCH
  ↺

That is the mechanical test of the concept: the planner can prefer an action that is locally worse because it changes the geometry of later actions enough to become globally better.

And once the Metamap learns that pattern, it has gained something more valuable than the answer to the original problem—it has gained a reusable rule for recognizing when the target behind the target matters more than the first one.